Starting point:
The primary suspect address is given in the case brief (Case Overview / Wallet Seizure Data). Begin from the output of the first "clean" transaction and work outward through the cluster.
Confirm your amount for Q2. This write-up records 12.47 BTC, but another public write-up of the same challenge reports a much smaller total (on the order of 0.0189 BTC) built from several sub-0.01 BTC inputs. Either the instance values are randomized per spawn or one figure is a transcription error — verify the total against your own Transaction Analysis view before publishing a number.

Methodology notes:
The investigation follows the classic money-trail sequence:
Start broad — graph the full address cluster (don't trace transactions one by one in a raw explorer; clustering is what makes the links visible).
Quantify impact — sum victim flows, watching decimal places carefully.
Identify evasion — detect the mixing signature; CoinJoin breaks naive hop-by-hop tracing.
Pivot to identity — a mixer is not the end of the trail; exchange KYC data collected at registration links the pseudonymous address to a person.
Correlate off-chain — resolve the KYC identity to a name and a location.

Takeaways:
Why pseudonymity fails here. 
Bitcoin is pseudonymous, not anonymous. The public ledger plus a single KYC touchpoint at a centralized exchange is enough to collapse an address cluster onto a real identity. 
A mixer slows tracing but does not defeat it once the funds pass through a KYC-gated service.
Detection (compliance / blue-team side). In production, this trail is caught by exchange-side KYT alerts on clustered, high-velocity transfers; analytics platforms flagging CoinJoin participation; 
and correlation between on-chain activity and the KYC records held at the point of fiat on/off-ramp.

